Appendix: Annotation Coordinate System Revamp
Current System — Absolute Pixels at Scale 1.0
All annotation coordinates (rect, anchor, knee, paths, points) are stored as absolute pixel values relative to the PDF page rendered at scale = 1.0. The rendering formula is simply:
Code
Coordinates are created by dividing client pointer positions by the current scale factor:
Code
Problems with this approach:
DPR coupling — PDF.js honours devicePixelRatio in its viewport. A page opened on a 2× display at zoom=100 produces a canvas that is twice as wide in CSS pixels, but the viewport scale=1.0 still yields the same logical pixel dimensions in PDF.js. If this assumption ever shifts (e.g., explicit DPR-aware rendering), all stored coordinates become wrong by a constant factor.
No page-dimension record — annotations don't store the page dimensions they were created against. There is no way to verify whether a stored coordinate is valid for its page without reloading the PDF. pageRemapper.ts works around this by receiving page dimensions as parameters, but the dimensions come from a live PDF.js call — they aren't stored.
Rotation is destructive — pageRemapper.ts:105–154 transforms coordinates and writes the rotated values back. This is irreversible without storing the original. A normalize-then-rotate model would be reversible.
Ink path arrays are untyped flat arrays — paths: number[][] and points: number[] mix x/y implicitly by index convention. No type enforces [x, y, x, y, ...] ordering.
No cross-page portability — if a page is replaced with a PDF of different page dimensions, existing annotations are not rescaled. They silently render in the wrong position with no error.
Proposed: Normalized (Fractional) Coordinate Model
Replace all stored coordinates with unit-fraction values in [0, 1] relative to the unscaled page dimensions (width and height from PDFPage.getViewport({ scale: 1 })).
